At pos=28 in the Messerene pattern, the code embeds the Bitcoin genesis nonce (2083236893) as 4 little-endian bytes into the last 4 positions of the
32-byte pattern.

What's at position 28
The Messerene pattern is 32 bytes long (indices 0–31). The genesis nonce 2083236893 is 0x7C2BAC1D, packed as little-endian is:

struct.pack('<I', 2083236893) = 0x1D 0xAC 0x2B 0x7C

At pos=28, these 4 bytes replace the last 4 bytes of the pattern (indices 28, 29, 30, 31):

╭──────────┬───────────────────────┬───────────────────────╮
│ Position │ Original pattern byte │ Replaced with (nonce) │
├──────────┼───────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
│ 28 │ 0xB3 │ 0x1D (nonce byte 0) │
│ 29 │ 0xC5 │ 0xAC (nonce byte 1) │
│ 30 │ 0xD7 │ 0x2B (nonce byte 2) │
│ 31 │ 0xE9 │ 0x7C (nonce byte 3) │
╰──────────┴───────────────────────┴───────────────────────╯

The original last 4 bytes of the pattern are B3C5D7E9. After embedding the nonce, they become 1DAC2B7C.

Why it crashed
The original code had no bounds check when writing the 4-byte nonce into the pattern. At pos=28, writing 4 bytes means accessing indices 28, 29, 30, 31
— which exactly fits a 32-byte array. The crash occurred because the pattern string used by the code
A1B3C5D7E9F8D6C4B2A0E8F6D4C2B0A8E6F4D2C0B8A6E4D2C0B8A6E4D2C0 is 60 hex characters, which is 30 bytes (not 32). When converted to bytes, orig_bytes has
length 30, so indices 30 and 31 are out of range — only 2 of the 4 nonce bytes (indices 28, 29) land.

Note: that string is also not the canonical pattern. The canonical pattern in messerene_operator.py is
A1B3C5D7E9F8D6C4B2A1B3C5D7E9F8D6C4B2A1B3C5D7E9F8D6C4B2A1B3C5D7E9 — 64 hex characters, 32 bytes, block "A1B3C5D7E9F8D6C4B2" x3 + "A1B3C5D7E9". On the canonical pattern, pos=28 + 4 bytes fits exactly and nothing is dropped. D2C0B8A6 is the repeating motif inside the short string, not its last 4 bytes — the short string's last 4 bytes are A6E4D2C0.

The fix added a bounds guard:

if pos + i < len(combined):
    combined[pos + i] = b

This means at pos=28 on a 30-byte array, only 2 of the 4 nonce bytes are written (indices 28, 29), and the last 2 bytes are silently dropped. On the canonical 32-byte pattern the guard changes nothing — all 4 bytes land.

